      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62077,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Tumours are usually unilateral but rarely may be bilateral {2823622}. Although presentation may be with symptoms related to an ovarian mass, patients frequently present with endocrine manifestations. About half of patients present with androgenic symptoms and 10% with estrogenic symptoms, and in rare cases there are progestational effects or Cushing syndrome {2823622}. Occasional steroid cell tumours have been reported in patients with von Hippel&ndash;Lindau syndrome {20664446; 30700659}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T19:05:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62078,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The average patient age is 43&nbsp;years {2823622}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62079,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62080,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are presumed to be of ovarian stromal cell origin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62081,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours have a mean diameter of about 8.4&nbsp;cm. The cut surface is solid and may show areas of haemorrhage and necrosis; it is typically yellow, orange, red, brown, or black {2823622}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62082,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours typically comprise an expansile diffuse growth of large polygonal cells, but nests, cords, and pseudoglandular and follicle-like arrangements of tumour cells may also be seen. Stroma ranges from scant to prominent with fibrous bands, rarely with areas morphologically in keeping with fibroma {29369920}. The tumour cells have abundant cytoplasm that ranges from eosinophilic (lipid-poor) to pale and vacuolated (lipid-rich). Variable amounts of intracytoplasmic lipochrome pigment can be seen. The nuclei are typically round, with a prominent central nucleolus; substantial nuclear atypia and necrosis are sometimes present, usually accompanied by increased mitotic activity. Prediction of malignant behaviour on the basis of pathological features is difficult, but factors predicting malignant behaviour include size &gt;&nbsp;7&nbsp;cm, significant mitotic activity, necrosis, haemorrhage, and significant nuclear atypia {2823622}. In some steroid cell tumours, in particular small tumours, stromal hyperthecosis may be seen in the adjacent ovarian stroma and contralateral ovary {2823622}. Immunohistochemically, steroid cell tumours are typically positive for sex cord&ndash;stromal markers, such as inhibin {9688187}, calretinin {11603218}, and melan-A {20567142}, but they are usually negative for FOXL2 {21378549}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62083,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 75,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:09:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
